{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** The spinal cord is the elongated, cylindrical part of the central nervous system below the head. In an adult it runs from the upper border of the atlas to the lower border of L1, ending as the conus medullaris. It gives rise to 31 pairs of spinal nerves, is wrapped in three meninges, and is supplied by one anterior and two posterior spinal arteries reinforced by radicular arteries.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="AN57.1, AN57.2, AN57.3, AN57.4, AN57.5" phase="Phase 1" /%}

## What is the spinal cord?

The spinal cord is the long, cylindrical part of the central nervous system that lies below the head. Above, it is continuous with the **medulla oblongata** just below the foramen magnum, at the upper border of the atlas. Below, it ends as a cone called the **conus medullaris**.

It does three jobs:

- It carries sensory information in from the body and sends motor axons out to skeletal muscle.
- It contains the cell bodies of the preganglionic neurones of the autonomic system, sympathetic and parasympathetic.
- It runs reflexes. The circuits for movements such as the to-and-fro movement of the legs in walking are in the cord. The brain only sends command signals to set them going.

Ascending and descending pathways link the cord with the brain, so higher centres can monitor what is happening and modulate the motor output.

## Extent of the spinal cord (AN57.2)

In an adult the cord extends from the upper border of the atlas to the lower border of the first lumbar vertebra. It is about 45 cm long. In a child it extends down to about L3.

The reason is growth. In the first-trimester fetus the cord runs the whole length of the vertebral column. After that the vertebral column lengthens more than the cord, so the lower end of the cord moves up relative to the bones.

{% table %}
- **Age**
- **Lower end of the spinal cord**
---
- **Early fetus**
- Runs to the end of the vertebral column
---
- **Birth**
- About the L3 vertebral level
---
- **Adult**
- Typically the L1 level
{% /table %}

The adult level varies. In some people the cord may end as high as T12, and in some it may reach L3. Flexion of the vertebral column moves the tip of the cord slightly upward.

Because the cord is much shorter than the vertebral column, the lower spinal nerve roots have to run downward before they reach their own intervertebral foramina. These long roots below the conus form the **cauda equina** ("horse's tail"). The pia mater continues from the tip of the cord as the **filum terminale**, which anchors the cord below, ending attached to the coccyx.

## External features (AN57.1)

- The cord is oval or round in section and **enlarged in the cervical and lumbar regions**, where the neurons for the upper and lower limbs are located.
- The **cervical enlargement** runs from C3 to T2 and supplies the upper limbs.
- The **lumbar enlargement** runs from L1 to S3 and supplies the lower limbs. Below it the cord dwindles rapidly into the conus medullaris.
- In front there is a deep **anterior median fissure**, which holds the anterior spinal artery.
- Behind there is a thin **posterior median sulcus**, from which a septum runs into the depth of the cord.
- Each half of the cord has an **anterolateral sulcus**, where the ventral (motor) roots emerge, and a **posterolateral sulcus**, where the dorsal (sensory) roots enter.

The cord is wider from side to side than from front to back at all levels, especially in the cervical segments.

## Segments and spinal nerves

The cord gives rise to **31 pairs of spinal nerves**: 8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral and 1 coccygeal. The part of the cord that gives rise to all the rootlets of one spinal nerve is a **cord segment**. The skin area supplied by one spinal nerve and its cord segment is a **dermatome**.

Each spinal nerve is formed by the merging of an anterior (ventral) root and a posterior (dorsal) root. The roots are made of smaller rootlets. The dorsal root has a swelling, the dorsal root ganglion. The roots join in the intervertebral foramen to form a **mixed spinal nerve**, which then divides into:

- A smaller **posterior primary ramus**, supplying the skin of the back and the muscles beside the spine.
- An **anterior primary ramus**, supplying all other structures of the body. In the cervical and lumbar enlargements these rami form the brachial and lumbosacral plexuses for the limbs. In the thorax they continue as the intercostal nerves.

The **Bell-Magendie law** says that anterior roots are motor and posterior roots are sensory. This is true to a good approximation. Roots and rootlets carry almost entirely motor or almost entirely sensory fibres (a few exceptions exist), while spinal nerves and their rami carry both. This is why a lesion of the anterior roots affects only motor fibres, but a lesion of the anterior rami affects both motor and sensory fibres.

**Where the nerves exit.** There are eight cervical segments but only seven cervical vertebrae. So the first seven cervical nerves leave above the same-numbered vertebra, and C8 leaves between C7 and T1. From there on, every nerve leaves below the vertebra of the same number. For example, L2 emerges between L2 and L3.

## Spinal segments versus vertebral levels

A vertebral spine is always lower than the matching spinal segment. This matters when you estimate a segment from the surface.

{% table %}
- **Vertebral levels**
- **Spinal segments**
---
- **C1 to C7**
- C1 to C8
---
- **T1 to T6**
- T1 to T8
---
- **T7 to T9**
- T9 to T12
---
- **T10 to T11**
- L1 to L5
---
- **T12 to L1**
- S1 to S5 and the coccygeal segment
{% /table %}

A working rule for palpation in the adult: in the cervical region, a spinous process lies opposite the next segment (the C6 spine is opposite the C7 segment). In the upper thoracic region the spine is opposite the segment two lower (T4 spine, T6 segment). In the lower thoracic region the difference is three segments (T10 spine, L1 segment). The T11 spine overlies the L3 segment, and the T12 spine is opposite S1.

In spinal injury the level of cord damage will not coincide with the level of damage to the bones.

## Meninges and spaces (AN56.1)

Three meninges surround the cord, as they do the brain:

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/spinal-cord/spinal-meninges.webp" alt="Diagram of the spinal meninges and the epidural and subarachnoid spaces." caption="The dura, arachnoid and pia surround the cord, with CSF in the subarachnoid space." credit="Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons" license="Public domain" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gray770-en.svg" width="960" height="378" /%}

1. **Dura mater:** the outermost, a dense, strong fibrous sheet that encloses the cord and cauda equina loosely like a sleeve. It is continuous above with the dura of the brain through the foramen magnum. Below, it ends on the filum terminale at the level of the second sacral vertebra. Laterally it extends along each nerve root and becomes continuous with the epineurium of the spinal nerve.
2. **Arachnoid mater:** the middle layer.
3. **Pia mater:** the innermost layer, closely applied to the cord and passing into the anterior median fissure.

{% table %}
- **Space**
- **Position and contents**
---
- **Epidural (extradural) space**
- Between the dura and the wall of the vertebral canal. Contains loose connective tissue, fat, the internal vertebral venous plexus, and small arteries, nerves and lymphatics.
---
- **Subdural space**
- Between the dura and the arachnoid
---
- **Subarachnoid space**
- Between the arachnoid and the pia. Contains cerebrospinal fluid (CSF).
{% /table %}

The pia gives rise to the **denticulate (dentate) ligaments**: thin bands that run from the side of the cord through the arachnoid to the dura. They suspend the cord within the meninges. The ligament is a flat fibrous sheet on each side between the ventral and dorsal roots, and its lateral border forms triangular processes fixed at intervals to the dura. The last one lies between the twelfth thoracic and first lumbar nerves.

## Lumbar puncture

In **lumbar puncture** (spinal tap), a needle is passed into the subarachnoid space in the low lumbar region to draw off CSF. The anatomy explains the level.

- In an adult the cord ends at about L1, so the puncture is done at the **L3** level, which avoids injury to the cord.
- In a child the cord extends to about L3, so the puncture is done lower, at the **L4** level.

The subarachnoid space around the filum terminale is wide, which makes it the best place to reach the CSF.

## Internal structure (AN57.3)

On cross-section the cord has an **H-shaped or butterfly-shaped core of grey matter** surrounded by **white matter**. The white matter is made of ascending and descending nerve fibres, mostly myelinated. The proportion of white to grey matter changes with the level. The absolute amount of white matter is greatest at cervical levels and falls lower down, because descending tracts shed fibres as they descend and ascending tracts gather fibres as they ascend.

In the centre of the grey matter is the **central canal**. It is lined by ependymal cells and contains CSF. It runs the whole length of the cord and a short distance into the filum terminale. Above, it extends into the lower half of the medulla and opens into the fourth ventricle. The two halves of the cord are joined by the grey commissure, with anterior and posterior white commissures around it.

### Grey matter: the horns

- **Posterior (dorsal) horn:** present at all levels. It contains sensory neurons that receive and process incoming somatosensory information and pass it upward.
- **Anterior (ventral) horn:** present at all levels. It contains the motor neurons.
- **Lateral horn:** present **only in T1 to L2 and S2 to S4**. It is part of the autonomic nervous system and supplies the viscera.

The anterior horn holds two kinds of motor neuron. **Alpha motor neurons** supply the large skeletal muscle fibres, and one alpha fibre with the muscle fibres it excites forms a motor unit. About half as many smaller **gamma motor neurons** supply the intrafusal fibres in the middle of the muscle spindle, which helps control muscle tone. **Interneurons** are found in all areas of the grey matter and are about 30 times as numerous as the anterior motor neurons.

### White matter: the funiculi

The white matter is divided into three columns on each side:

- **Posterior funiculus:** from the posterior median sulcus to the attachment of the posterior rootlets. In the upper cord a posterior intermediate sulcus separates the medial fasciculus gracilis from the lateral fasciculus cuneatus.
- **Lateral funiculus:** between the attachments of the posterior and anterior rootlets.
- **Anterior funiculus:** from the anterior rootlets to the anterior median fissure.

## Autonomic outflow from the cord

- **Sympathetic outflow** comes from the intermediolateral grey matter of **T1 to L2**. These preganglionic fibres leave through the ventral root and spinal nerve, pass as the white ramus communicans to the sympathetic ganglion, and are relayed. The ganglion sends a grey ramus communicans back to the spinal nerve.
- **Parasympathetic outflow** comes from **S2 to S4** and supplies the pelvic viscera.

## Tracts (AN57.4)

Only a few tract facts are firm in this lesson. For the full list of ascending and descending tracts at mid-thoracic level, use your department's notes.

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/spinal-cord/spinal-tracts.webp" alt="Cross section of the spinal cord showing ascending and descending tracts." caption="Tracts are grouped in the white matter columns by function." credit="Polarlys and Mikael Häggström, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 3.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spinal_cord_tracts_-_English.svg" width="960" height="414" /%}

- **Corticospinal fibres cross to the opposite side:** 80% cross in the pyramidal decussation, 15% cross in the spinal cord and 5% do not cross.
- Of the six main ascending tracts, two go to the cerebellum, two relay in nuclei of the spinal cord and reach the opposite side, and two relay in nuclei in the medulla oblongata and reach the opposite side.
- **Lamination:** in the dorsal columns the legs are medial and the arms lateral. In the corticospinal and anterolateral tracts the arms are medial and the legs lateral. This pattern is important for understanding central cord syndrome, for example in syringomyelia (AN57.5).

## Blood supply of the spinal cord

The cord is supplied by **three longitudinal arteries**: one **anterior spinal artery** and two **posterior spinal arteries**. They typically arise from the distal vertebral arteries. They form longitudinal arterial plexuses along the whole length of the cord, and are reinforced at each level by **radicular arteries** that follow the dorsal and ventral nerve roots.

- The anterior spinal artery runs down in the anterior median fissure. It gives off central (sulcocommissural) branches that supply most of the anterior grey columns and part of the posterior grey columns.
- The posterior spinal arteries run down close to the attachments of the posterior rootlets.
- The ventral radicular branches that join to form the anterior spinal artery are also called radiculomedullary arteries. In adults, five to eight of these radicular arteries persist as the dominant ones.

### Regional supply of the anterior spinal artery

{% table %}
- **Region**
- **Supply**
---
- **Upper four cervical segments**
- Descending anterior spinal artery, formed from branches of the intracranial part of the vertebral arteries
---
- **Lower half of cervical cord and upper two thoracic segments**
- Two to four anterior radicular vessels from the vertebral, deep cervical and superior intercostal arteries
---
- **Mid-thoracic region**
- Usually a single anterior radicular branch from an adjacent intercostal artery. This is a watershed area.
---
- **Lower thoracic, lumbar and sacral region**
- Mainly the **artery of Adamkiewicz**
{% /table %}

### The artery of Adamkiewicz

This is the largest anterior radiculomedullary artery. It arises from the aorta, most often from a left posterior intercostal artery, most commonly the left tenth. It may arise anywhere between the eighth dorsal and fourth lumbar level. It supplies the lower two-thirds of the cord. If its origin is relatively high or low, there is usually a supplementary vessel to the anterior spinal artery.

Small vessels that accompany the lumbar and sacral roots form an anastomosis around the conus medullaris. This can supply the lower cord if the artery of Adamkiewicz is blocked.

### Clinical points

- The anterior spinal artery is single, while the posterior cord has two posterior spinal arteries and so more collateral flow. The **anterior part of the cord is therefore more susceptible to ischaemia**.
- The territory of the artery of Adamkiewicz is supplied by one major artery, so it is vulnerable to watershed ischaemia.
- Thrombotic occlusion of the artery of Adamkiewicz can cause ischaemic change in the thoracic cord.
- The precise area supplied by any one radiculomedullary artery varies greatly, so one cannot predict how much cord will be infarcted if one is blocked.

The veins of the cord form a network that drains into the internal vertebral venous plexus.

## Applied anatomy (AN57.2, AN57.5)

{% table %}
- **Condition**
- **Anatomical basis**
- **Features**
---
- **Conus medullaris syndrome**
- Injury to S2 to S4 segments
- Anaesthesia of the perineum. Bladder and bowel involvement is **early**, because S2 to S4 carry the sacral parasympathetic supply to the bladder and lower bowel. Sexual function is affected.
---
- **Cauda equina syndrome**
- Damage to the bundle of lumbosacral roots below the cord
- Lower motor neuron paralysis of the lower limbs, from compression of the ventral roots. Root pain from dorsal root involvement. Bladder and bowel involvement is **late**.
---
- **Poliomyelitis**
- Virus affects the anterior horn cells
- Flaccid paralysis. Vaccination with polio drops has eradicated the disease.
---
- **Brown-Sequard syndrome**
- Injury to one side of the cord, for example from penetrating trauma
- Ipsilateral loss of motor function (injury to the ipsilateral corticospinal tract and posterior columns), and contralateral loss of pain and temperature (injury to the lateral spinothalamic tract)
---
- **Central cord syndrome**
- Injury to the central grey matter, usually from hyperextension of an osteoarthritic neck
- Incomplete injury with greater motor loss in the upper limbs than the lower limbs
---
- **Anterior cord syndrome**
- Damage in the territory of the anterior spinal artery, for example in flexion-compression injuries of the neck
- Posterior columns spared. Motor loss usually greater in the lower limbs than the upper, with less sensory loss.
{% /table %}

The cauda equina is less easily injured than the cord in the neck and thorax because the lumbar canal is relatively larger. At C1 the cord occupies about one-third of the canal, and in the lower cervical and thoracic region about 50%.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Conus versus cauda equina:** conus medullaris syndrome is a cord injury (S2 to S4) with **early** bladder and bowel loss. Cauda equina syndrome is a root injury with root pain and **late** bladder and bowel loss.
{% /callout %}

## Mnemonics

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Spine below segment:** a vertebral spine is always lower than its spinal segment. The cord ends at L1 in an adult and L3 in a child, so the lumbar puncture goes in lower: L3 in the adult, L4 in the child.
{% /callout %}

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The adult cord typically ends at the level of L1, as the vertebral column grows longer than the cord during development. At birth it ends at about L3." %}
In an adult, the spinal cord typically ends at the lower border of which vertebra?
- A. T12
- B. L3
- C. L1
- D. S2
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="In the adult the cord ends at about L1, so the needle is placed at the L3 level. In a child the cord reaches about L3, so the puncture is done lower, at L4." %}
Lumbar puncture in an adult is done at the L3 level mainly because:
- A. The subarachnoid space is absent above L3
- B. The spinal cord ends at about L1, so the needle avoids it
- C. The dura mater ends at L3
- D. The cauda equina is absent at L3
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="The lateral horn is present only in T1 to L2 and S2 to S4, where it holds autonomic preganglionic neurons supplying the viscera." %}
The lateral grey horn of the spinal cord is present in:
- A. All segments of the cord
- B. C1 to C8 only
- C. L3 to S1 only
- D. T1 to L2 and S2 to S4
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="The artery of Adamkiewicz is the largest anterior radiculomedullary artery. It supplies the lower two-thirds of the cord, so it is the main supply of the lower thoracic, lumbar and sacral cord." %}
The lower thoracic, lumbar and sacral parts of the spinal cord are supplied mainly by:
- A. The artery of Adamkiewicz
- B. The posterior spinal arteries alone
- C. The ascending cervical artery
- D. The descending anterior spinal artery from the vertebral arteries
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The conus medullaris carries the S2 to S4 segments, which hold the sacral parasympathetic supply to the bladder and lower bowel. In cauda equina syndrome, bladder and bowel involvement comes late." %}
Early loss of bladder and bowel control with perineal anaesthesia suggests damage to:
- A. The cauda equina
- B. The conus medullaris (S2 to S4 segments)
- C. The lumbar enlargement
- D. The cervical enlargement
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The anterior spinal artery is single, while the posterior cord has two posterior spinal arteries and more collateral flow. So the anterior part of the cord is more susceptible to ischaemia." %}
Which part of the cord is more susceptible to ischaemia, and why?
- A. The posterior cord, because the posterior arteries are small
- B. The lateral cord, because it has no arterial supply
- C. The anterior cord, because it depends on a single anterior spinal artery
- D. The central canal, because it is lined by ependyma
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="Where does the spinal cord begin and end in an adult and in a child?" %}
It begins at the upper border of the atlas, continuous with the medulla oblongata. It ends at the lower border of L1 in an adult and at about L3 in a child, as the conus medullaris.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Why does the spinal cord end higher than the vertebral column?" %}
After the first trimester the vertebral column lengthens more than the spinal cord. The cord appears to ascend, and the lower nerve roots lengthen to form the cauda equina.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Name the meninges and the spaces between them." %}
Dura mater (outer), arachnoid mater (middle) and pia mater (inner). The epidural space lies outside the dura, the subdural space between dura and arachnoid, and the subarachnoid space, which holds CSF, between arachnoid and pia.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="How many spinal nerves are there, and how do they exit?" %}
31 pairs: 8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral and 1 coccygeal. C1 to C7 exit above the same-numbered vertebra, C8 between C7 and T1, and all the rest below the same-numbered vertebra.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Where is the lateral horn found, and what does it do?" %}
Only in T1 to L2 and S2 to S4. It is part of the autonomic system and supplies the viscera: sympathetic from T1 to L2, parasympathetic from S2 to S4.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Describe the arterial supply of the spinal cord." %}
One anterior and two posterior spinal arteries run along the cord, reinforced by radicular arteries. The artery of Adamkiewicz supplies the lower two-thirds of the cord.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Differentiate conus medullaris syndrome from cauda equina syndrome." %}
Conus syndrome is injury to S2 to S4 with perineal anaesthesia and early bladder and bowel involvement. Cauda equina syndrome is a root injury with lower motor neuron paralysis, root pain and late bladder and bowel involvement.
{% /viva %}

